  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain moves to the south early tonight, but \nreturns for Monday. Flood watch considered. \n\nNebulous/broad sfc low pressure over the CWA at 18Z, seemingly\ncentered somewhere within 40 miles of UNV. Several short wave \ntroughs continue to roll into and over the Mid-Atlantic, but \nnone have instability to work with north of the Mason-Dixon Line.\nPWATs remain high, touching the 2\" mark in York/Lanc Cos. Flow \nis westerly and constant, but slightly confluent aloft. The \nclosest thunder at 18Z is in ern OH and dropping steadily south \nenough that it will likely miss our CWA. But, occasional \nshowers/rain continue to move in from upstream. QPF still looks \nlow enough (<0.75\") through midnight in the Laurels where the \nFFG is lowest (just under an inch in some places). That should \nbe low enough in intensity/duration to keep any hydo problems\nminor and isolated. After collaboration with neighboring \noffices, we had low enough confidence in flooding-intensity\nrainfall to stay away from a Flood Watch at this point in time.\nBut, the Laurels (particiularly Somerset Co) would be the place\nmost-susceptible to any flooding into late this evening. \nConditions are too dry farther east, and the bulk of the rain \nshould stay south of I-80 for the rest of the day. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Rain potential continues for much of this week, \nalthough uncertainty remains in the details due to offshore low \ndevelopment possibilities. On the cool side as well.\n\nA fairly strong short wave trough currently over rolls in from\nlower LM late tonight. That should yield a renewal of rainfall\nin the Laurels. The system will still be working with 1.5-2.0\" \nPWATs. But, it will arriving at an even more-stable time of day.\nThe steady east and slightly northward spread of precip will\nonly reinforce the stability (and keep temps chilly) Monday.\nAgain, thunder cannot be ruled out, but would likely be\nelevated. It may remain dry (or just patchy drizzle) across the\nnorthern tier for much of the day. Models funnel the best\nmoisture into the eastern half of PA Monday night, easing the \nflooding potential somewhat for the Laurels.\n\nExtremely blocky pattern aloft yeilds very low confidence in sfc\nfeatures and precip potential/placement beyond Tuesday. Low\npressure tries to develop off shore (along the Gulf Stream) Wed,\nbut may be shunted eastward through the second half of the \nweek. The sure bet this week is for below normal max temps.",
